# nomArmy subscription-backed individual workers -- OPTIONAL.
#
# The easy path is `nomarmy subscriptions setup` -- it installs the vendor
# CLI if needed, runs its login, handles OpenClaw's plugin, lists the real
# models to choose from, and proves the credential with a real test call
# before writing this file for you. Copying this file by hand is the
# lower-level route for a credential you've already set up yourself.
# Absent entirely, nothing changes -- this feature is fully additive.
#
# A worker here is backed by ONE SPECIFIC PERSON'S OWN already-authenticated
# subscription (a Claude Pro/Max/Team seat, or an OpenAI ChatGPT plan via
# Codex) -- never a shared credential, never pooled/interchangeable capacity
# the way config/providers.yml's weighted pools are. Dispatched with
# `subscription_worker: "<name>"` (or `subscription_role: "<role>"`) plus a
# REQUIRED `on_behalf_of` naming the exact person this job is for. nomArmy
# refuses the job -- never substitutes a different worker -- if on_behalf_of
# is missing or doesn't match this entry's own declared `owner`.
#
# nomArmy never touches a token for these. If you set one up by hand:
#
#   Claude (Pro, Max, or a Team-plan seat -- the Agent SDK credit this uses
#   is per-user/per-seat and non-transferable across all of them):
#     claude auth login                # OpenClaw reuses this session directly
#     openclaw models list --refresh   # real model ids, e.g. claude-cli/claude-sonnet-5
#
#   OpenAI (ChatGPT plan, via Codex):
#     nomarmy subscriptions setup codex
#   which does: npm install -g @openai/codex && codex login, installs
#   clawhub:@openclaw/codex (needs OpenClaw >= 2026.9.5), and runs
#   `openclaw models auth login --provider codex`. That imports the login as
#   an openai/oauth profile, so the worker's provider is "openai", not "codex".
#
#   Meta (Muse Code subscription):
#     muse login
#     nomarmy subscriptions setup meta
#   Meta's plugin (@openclaw/meta-provider) only takes an API key, but Muse
#   Code's own login mints a subscription-covered one into the macOS
#   keychain; setup copies that key into OpenClaw over stdin. A key you
#   create by hand on Meta's site bills pay-as-you-go instead.
#
# One OpenClaw provider id holds one credential. OpenAI ("openai"), Meta
# ("meta") and xAI ("xai") use the same id for the subscription and the API key, so a
# config/providers.yml pool entry on the same provider makes nomArmy refuse
# to dispatch either side until one is removed.
#
# Every entry needs: provider (the OpenClaw provider id -- "claude-cli",
# "openai" or "meta"), model (an id from `openclaw models list --refresh` for that
# provider), and owner (exactly who this credential belongs to -- checked
# against every dispatched job's on_behalf_of). Optional: role (unique
# across entries; a fixed name a job can dispatch by instead of the worker
# name), max_concurrent (default 1, not config/providers.yml's 2 -- a
# personal session was never rate-provisioned for concurrent automated
# jobs), thinking (default true; a specific level -- low/medium/high -- is
# this entry's own fixed floor regardless of what a job requests; false
# disables it), and context_window (only if OpenClaw's catalog doesn't know
# the model's window yet).

workers:
  you-claude:
    provider: claude-cli
    model: claude-sonnet-5
    owner: "you@example.com"
    role: senior-dev
    max_concurrent: 1
    thinking: true

  # you-codex:
  #   provider: openai
  #   model: gpt-6-astra
  #   owner: "you@example.com"
  #   role: ui-ux
